name: file descriptors
intro: Under `FILE *` and `fopen` sits the real interface: a file descriptor,
intro: a small int the kernel gives you. 0 is standard input, 1 standard
intro: output, 2 standard error. This is the unix layer; it needs
intro: `#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L` before the includes, plus
intro: <unistd.h> and <fcntl.h>.
intro:
intro:     int fd = open(path, O_RDONLY);               /* -1 on error */
intro:     int out = open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);
intro:     ssize_t n = read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);   /* 0 = end, -1 = error */
intro:     ssize_t w = write(out, buf, n);          /* bytes written */
intro:     close(fd);
intro:
intro: `O_CREAT` makes the file if missing; the third argument is then its
intro: permission bits (octal; the process umask clears some). `O_TRUNC`
intro: empties an existing file the moment you open it -- so copying a file
intro: onto itself destroys it before the first read. Check for that.
intro:
intro: `read` and `write` do not promise to move what you asked. A read from
intro: a pipe returns whatever has arrived: 3 bytes when you asked for 4096.
intro: A write can take only part of a big buffer. Both can fail with
intro: `EINTR`, a signal arriving mid-call; that is not an error, so call
intro: again. So you loop:
intro:
intro:     while (n > 0) {
intro:         ssize_t w = write(fd, p, n);
intro:         if (w < 0) { if (errno == EINTR) continue; return -1; }
intro:         p += w;  n -= (size_t)w;
intro:     }
intro:
intro: `read` returning 0 is end of file. Reading "exactly N bytes" means
intro: looping until you have N or see 0.
intro:
intro: To tell whether two paths or descriptors are one file, add
intro: `#include <sys/stat.h>` and call `struct stat st; fstat(fd, &st)`
intro: or `stat(path, &st)`. A file is identified by `st.st_dev` together
intro: with `st.st_ino`.
intro:
intro: stdio sits on top and keeps its own buffer. `printf` to a pipe or file
intro: does not write until the buffer fills or the program ends, but
intro: `write(1, ...)` goes at once. Mix the two on one fd and the output
intro: reorders. Call `fflush(stdout)` before a raw write, or use only one
intro: layer for a given fd. Close what you open, and check `close` on a file
intro: you wrote: some errors only show there.
---
q: recs4.c -- read exact 4-byte records from a pipe
spec: Read standard input as records of exactly 4 bytes using `read` on file
spec: descriptor 0 -- not stdio. Print each record as 8 lowercase hex digits
spec: on its own line.
spec:
spec: A pipe may hand over fewer bytes than you ask for, so a record can
spec: arrive in pieces; keep reading until you have all 4 or the input ends.
spec: If the input ends partway through a record, print `partial N` (N the
spec: bytes of it) and exit 1. A read error prints `recs4: read error` to
spec: standard error and exits 1. Otherwise exit 0. Empty input prints
spec: nothing.
file: recs4.c
prog: #define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <unistd.h>
prog: 
prog: /* read exactly n bytes unless the stream ends first; returns how many */
prog: static ssize_t read_full(int fd, unsigned char *buf, size_t n)
prog: {
prog: 	size_t got = 0;
prog: 	while (got < n) {
prog: 		ssize_t r = read(fd, buf + got, n - got);
prog: 		if (r < 0) {
prog: 			if (errno == EINTR)
prog: 				continue;
prog: 			return -1;
prog: 		}
prog: 		if (r == 0)
prog: 			break;
prog: 		got += (size_t)r;
prog: 	}
prog: 	return (ssize_t)got;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	unsigned char rec[4];
prog: 	for (;;) {
prog: 		ssize_t n = read_full(0, rec, sizeof rec);
prog: 		if (n < 0) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "recs4: read error\n");
prog: 			return 1;
prog: 		}
prog: 		if (n == 0)
prog: 			return 0;
prog: 		if (n < (ssize_t)sizeof rec) {
prog: 			printf("partial %zd\n", n);
prog: 			return 1;
prog: 		}
prog: 		printf("%02x%02x%02x%02x\n", rec[0], rec[1], rec[2], rec[3]);
prog: 	}
prog: }
case: printf 'abcdefgh' | recs4
case: printf 'abcde' | recs4; echo $?
case: printf '\000\377\200\001' | recs4; printf '' | recs4
case: (printf ab; sleep 1; printf cdef) | recs4
hint: an inner loop that adds up what read returns until the count reaches 4, a zero return ends it early, and EINTR means try again
